  "title": "Go and Step: ced, cess, gress and grad",
  "short": "ced, ceed and cess mean go or yield; gress and grad mean step.",
  "trouble": "Three spellings, one root: -cede (concede), -ceed (proceed, succeed, exceed) and -cess (excess). supersede is the odd one out: it comes from a different root, sed (sit).",
  "prereqs": [
    "roots.pre-post",
    "roots.dict-loqu"
  ],
  "xp": 1,
  "viz": [
    "vo-roots",
    "vo-word"
  ],
  "figureArchetype": "GeneralFigure",
  "practice": null,
  "lesson": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "One root, three spellings: <b>ced</b>, <b>ceed</b> and <b>cess</b> all mean “go” (and sometimes “yield,” go along with). To <i>precede</i> is to go before, to <i>proceed</i> is to go forward and a <i>recess</i> is a going back, a break. Another root, <b>gress</b> (also <b>grad</b>), means “step”: <i>progress</i> is stepping forward, and a <i>gradual</i> change happens step by step."
      },
      {
