      {
        "t": "key",
        "html": "<b>ced</b>, <b>ceed</b>, <b>cess</b> = <b>go, yield</b>. <b>gress</b>, <b>grad</b> = <b>step, go</b>."
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "html": "Many <i>ced</i> words describe an order of events: an <b>antecedent</b> goes before, a <b>predecessor</b> went before you in a job, a <b>successor</b> comes after. Others describe giving way: to <b>concede</b> is to yield a point, and to <b>accede</b> is to go along with a request."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "The parts and their words"
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "root": "ced"
        },
        "cap": "Words on the list built on ced and cess."
      },
      {
        "t": "viz",
        "name": "vo-roots",
        "opts": {
          "max": 10,
          "root": "gress"
        },
        "cap": "gress and grad (step): familiar words."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "text": "Words taken apart"
      },
      {
        "t": "table",
        "head": [
          "Word and meaning",
          "Parts, and what they say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "<b>antecedent</b><br>something that comes before",
            "ante- <i>before</i> + <b>ced</b> <i>go</i> + -ent<br>→ “going before”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>successor</b><br>a person who follows another in a role",
            "suc- <i>after, next</i> + <b>cess</b> <i>go</i> + -or <i>one who</i><br>→ “one who goes next”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>concede</b><br>to admit that something is true, often reluctantly",
            "con- <i>completely</i> + <b>ced</b> <i>yield</i><br>→ “yield completely”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>accede</b><br>to agree to a request or demand",
            "ac- <i>to</i> + <b>ced</b> <i>go, yield</i><br>→ “go along to”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>excessive</b><br>more than is necessary",
            "ex- <i>out</i> + <b>cess</b> <i>go</i> + -ive<br>→ “going out (beyond the limit)”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>incessant</b><br>never stopping",
            "in- <i>not</i> + <b>cess</b> <i>stop</i> + -ant<br>→ “not stopping”"
          ],
          [
            "<b>cessation</b><br>a stop; an end",
            "<b>cess</b> <i>stop</i> + -ation<br>→ “a stopping”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>progress</i></b><br>movement forward or toward a goal",
            "pro- <i>forward</i> + <b>gress</b> <i>step</i><br>→ “a stepping forward”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>digress</i></b><br>to wander away from the main subject",
            "di- <i>apart, aside</i> + <b>gress</b> <i>step</i><br>→ “step aside”"
          ],
          [
            "<b><i>gradual</i></b><br>happening slowly, by small steps",
            "<b>grad</b> <i>step</i> + -ual<br>→ “step by step”"
          ]
        ],
        "cap": "Each word, what it means now, and its parts. Words in italics are familiar words that are not on the list."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "title": "Use the parts",
        "q": "The <b>incessant</b> rain kept the team indoors all week. What does <i>incessant</i> mean?",
        "steps": [
          "Take it apart: <b>in-</b> (not) + <b>cess</b> + <b>-ant</b>.",
          "Here <b>cess</b> comes from Latin <i>cessare</i>, “to stop” (a form of <i>cedere</i>, to go away, yield: the same root as <i>cease</i>).",
          "Literally: “not stopping.” The rain went on all week."
        ],
        "a": "Never stopping."
      },
